findings.description:
Line 87 says 300 persistence is per-run/per-project and that aggregation across projects is "a new requirement". It then defers whether to extend existing storage, add a store, or use 280, which isn't started. Slices 2–5 all depend on this choice, including the cross-project audit baseline. The document is silent on:
- where the data lives for a single-operator tool (a local file or SQLite versus a shared service);
- how project-local results are exported or ingested into it;
- what happens when a project's result files are pruned or moved.

Since 280 is explicitly "not assumed", pick a default (for example, a local queryable store) and list 280 as a migration path only. Otherwise the "keystone" slice is open-ended.

The document has no error strategy for several cases:
- an audit run that times out, errors, or produces unparseable prose (the normalization step is the hard, under-specified part, lines 83 and 96);
- partial cross-project runs;
- audit runs against projects at different commits, which breaks comparability;
- duplicate or conflicting human samples for the same result.

The "normalize into category, location, severity" step is described as a single bullet. Under a non-deterministic LLM markdown output, category taxonomy stability and finding de-duplication (a finding moving a few lines between runs) are the real cost. Normalization is the hidden complexity cliff for slice 4.
